{"status": "ok", "backend": "ddgs-auto", "query": "Normal ultrasound at 22 weeks with fetal demise at 35 weeks: next steps in management after stillbirth.", "retrieved_at": "2026-10-18T19:24:29.568051+00:00", "documents": [{"title": "Care of late intrauterine fetal death and stillbirth", "url": "https://obgyn.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1111/1471-0528.17844", "snippet": "Oct 28, 2024 · To identify evidence-based options for parents and their families who have a late intrauterine fetal death (IUFD) after 24 +0 completed weeks of pregnancy of a singleton fetus. To incorporate information on general care before, during and after birth, and care in future pregnancies."}, {"title": "Care in pregnancies subsequent to stillbirth or perinatal deathObstetric Care Consensus #10: Management of StillbirthSociety for Maternal-Fetal Medicine Special Statement ...IUFD / Stillbirth — Inpatient / L&D Order Set (Example)ACOG SMFM Obstetric Care Consensus #10: Management of StillbirthNo. 369-Management of Pregnancy Subsequent to Stillbirth", "url": "https://obgyn.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/tog.12708", "snippet": "Nov 4, 2020 · Antenatal care in a subsequent pregnancy after stillbirth should ideally be delivered by a multidisciplinary team to provide continuity of physical and psychological care. Jan 28, 2020 · The purpose of this document is to review the current information on stillbirth, including definitions and management, the evaluation of a stillbirth, and strategies for prevention. We present simple checklists for the care of patients with stillbirth designed for site-based adaptation and implementation. Confirm diagnosis with ultrasound documentation of absent fetal cardiac activity (per institutional standard; consider second confirmation if needed). Document: GA, presentation, placenta location, estimated interval since demise (if known), and maternal symptoms. The method and timing of delivery after a stillbirth depend on the gestational age at which the death occurred, maternal obstetric history (eg, previous hysterotomy), and maternal preference. Women and families presenting for care following a pregnancy affected by stillbirth or other causes perinatal loss. This document presents a summary of the literature and a general consensus on the management of pregnancies subsequent to stillbirth and perinatal loss."}, {"title": "Obstetric Care Consensus #10: Management of Stillbirth", "url": "https://www.ajog.org/article/S0002-9378(20)30024-7/fulltext", "snippet": "Jan 28, 2020 · The purpose of this document is to review the current information on stillbirth, including definitions and management, the evaluation of a stillbirth, and strategies for prevention."}]}
